-- =============================================================================
-- MoveApp — 018: match on real capacity, not on a class
-- =============================================================================
-- From the operator interview on 21 September.
--
-- About 80% of the time the client says how many people and how much luggage,
-- and the operator decides which car suits. The other 20% the client names a
-- car — and they can name anything. "Range Rover." "A BMW." "Something big."
--
-- That second case is what kills the idea of a fixed class list. A dropdown
-- can only hold words we thought of in advance, and the client is not reading
-- from our list. So the request is recorded as the client said it, and a human
-- decides what to do about it. That is what the operator does today on the
-- telephone, and it works.
--
-- Matching therefore moves to the numbers, which are the same in every
-- language: does this car seat that many people, and take that much luggage.
--
-- vehicle_classes and vehicles.vehicle_class_id are NOT dropped. They are left
-- in place, unused. Dropping a column is irreversible and this decision is two
-- days old; leaving it costs nothing and buys the right to change our minds.
--
-- Safe to run more than once.
-- =============================================================================

BEGIN;

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- What the client asked for, in their words
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Free text on purpose. "S Class", "Range Rover", "the big Mercedes", "same car
-- as last time" — all of these are real things clients say, and all of them are
-- useful to the operator. None of them would survive a dropdown.
--
-- This is shown prominently on the booking, never matched on.
ALTER TABLE bookings
    ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS requested_vehicle VARCHAR(120);

COMMENT ON COLUMN bookings.requested_vehicle IS
    'What the client asked for, verbatim. Display only — never used for matching.';

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Capacity on the vehicle
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- These columns already exist. What follows is documentation and an index,
-- because from today they carry the weight that vehicle_class_id used to.
--
-- Deliberately left NULLABLE. A car whose capacity nobody has filled in yet is
-- treated as "unknown", and unknown means SHOWN to the operator, flagged,
-- rather than hidden.
--
-- That direction matters. In September a free-text class mismatch made drivers
-- silently disappear from the assignment screen, with no error and no clue —
-- it took two hours to find. Hiding is the dangerous default. An operator who
-- can see a car with a question mark against its capacity will ask; an
-- operator who cannot see it at all will never know it was there.
COMMENT ON COLUMN vehicles.seats IS
    'Real passenger seats, excluding the driver. NULL means not yet recorded.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN vehicles.luggage_large IS
    'Large cases the boot takes. NULL means not yet recorded.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN vehicles.luggage_small IS
    'Small bags on top of the large ones. NULL means not yet recorded.';

-- Every assignment screen now filters on these three columns together with the
-- two status columns. One index covering the lot.
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS vehicles_capacity_lookup
    ON vehicles (verification_status, availability_status, seats);

COMMIT;


